What's included

File	Function
f1sh_linux_agent.conf	Monitors SSH, sudo, user accounts, cron jobs, systemd services, and executables

f1sh_windows_agent.conf	Monitors Windows startup locations, scheduled task files, hosts file, Group Policy files, and registry persistence

f1sh_fim_rules.xml	Four custom rules to prioritize critical Linux and Windows changes

README.md	Full installation, verification, and troubleshooting instructions

Step-by-step deployment on the Wazuh manager
All of the following commands run on the Wazuh manager, except the optional endpoint testing.
Step 1 — Check existing agents and groups
sudo /var/ossec/bin/agent_control -l
sudo /var/ossec/bin/agent_groups -l


Identify which agent IDs belong to Linux and Windows machines.
Step 2 — Create your F1.sh groups
sudo /var/ossec/bin/agent_groups -a -g f1sh-linux
sudo /var/ossec/bin/agent_groups -a -g f1sh-windows


Skip creating a group if it already exists.
Step 3 — Upload your files
Extract the ZIP onto the manager and copy the files to these locations:
Source file	Destination
f1sh_linux_agent.conf	/var/ossec/etc/shared/f1sh-linux/agent.conf
f1sh_windows_agent.conf	/var/ossec/etc/shared/f1sh-windows/agent.conf
f1sh_fim_rules.xml	/var/ossec/etc/rules/f1sh_fim_rules.xml

If the group configurations already exist, back them up and merge your settings rather than overwriting them.

For new, empty group configurations, you can use:
sudo cp f1sh_linux_agent.conf \
  /var/ossec/etc/shared/f1sh-linux/agent.conf

sudo cp f1sh_windows_agent.conf \
  /var/ossec/etc/shared/f1sh-windows/agent.conf

sudo cp f1sh_fim_rules.xml \
  /var/ossec/etc/rules/f1sh_fim_rules.xml


Step 4 — Assign existing agents
Replace the example IDs with actual IDs from Step 1.
# Example Linux agent
sudo /var/ossec/bin/agent_groups -a -i 001 -g f1sh-linux

# Example Windows agent
sudo /var/ossec/bin/agent_groups -a -i 002 -g f1sh-windows


Check agent_groups -h before running these commands. You want to preserve the existing group memberships, not accidentally replace the organizers' configurations.
Step 5 — Restart the manager
After checking that the rule IDs do not conflict with existing rules:
sudo systemctl restart wazuh-manager
sudo systemctl status wazuh-manager


Check for configuration errors:
sudo tail -n 80 /var/ossec/logs/ossec.log


Step 6 — Verify detection
In the Wazuh dashboard, open the File Integrity Monitoring section, select a connected agent, and inspect its events.

The ZIP includes instructions for enabling a harmless test directory so you can verify file creation, modification, and deletion without touching critical system files.